# Binsec/Rel constant-time check for aes_sbox (Tier B per ADR-0009).
#
# aes_sbox implements the AES forward S-box via the 113-gate
# Boyar-Peralta combinational circuit (per the asm spec block) — a
# branch-free, table-free arithmetic computation on the 8 input bits.
# The constant-time property is therefore "argued by construction"
# at the asm level; this Tier B proof discharges that argument
# against the compiled RV32IMC binary.
#
# Threat model: the input byte (a0, low 8 bits) is the secret. All
# other registers (sp, ra, callee-saved) are public. The output
# (a0) is permitted to depend on the secret (it must — that is the
# function's contract). What must NOT depend on the secret:
#   * Control flow (no branch on any bit of the secret).
#   * Memory access addresses (no load/store with secret-dependent
#     address — only stack spill/fill at fixed offsets is allowed).
#
# Setup:
#   * sp is fixed at a concrete safe address well above .text/.data.
#   * ra is set to a sentinel address; exploration halts there.
#   * a0 is a symbolic secret (low 8 bits; high bits zero).

starting from <aes_sbox> with
  sp := 0x90000000
  ra := 0xDEADBEEF
  a0 := secret as input_byte
  assume a0 < 256
end

# Halt at the function's return — Binsec interprets the `<symbol>
# return` form as "halt when control reaches the ret instruction in
# <symbol>", which is robust against asm address drift from edits
# elsewhere in the binary.
halt at <aes_sbox> return

explore all
